<!--
  The startup task the packaged build needs, and the only way an app with package
  identity can start with Windows.

  Windows ignores the per-user Run key for a packaged app: what launches at sign-in is
  a task the manifest declares, and the person can then switch it on or off in
  Settings, Apps, Startup (or Task Manager, Startup apps). Without this declaration the
  checkbox in the settings window has nothing to write to and silently does nothing.

  electron-builder appends this file inside the application's Extensions element
  (appx.customExtensionsPath), so it must not carry a root element of its own, and the
  values below are literal rather than macros: the substitution pass runs over the
  manifest template before this file is inserted. Executable has to match the
  Application element electron-builder writes, which is app, backslash, product name.

  Enabled="false" is deliberate: "start with Windows" is off by default in this app,
  and the settings window is what turns it on (src/main/index.js, which reaches this
  task through WinRT). TaskId appears there too, and the two have to agree: it is the
  only handle Windows gives the app on the task, and a mismatch looks exactly like a
  build that never declared it.

  No Arguments attribute: the schema gives a startup task TaskId, Enabled, DisplayName
  and ImmediateRegistration, nothing else, and makeappx refuses the package if one is
  added. So a launch through this task cannot be told apart from a manual one at start,
  unlike the login item the unpackaged build writes, which appends the autostart marker
  argument the main process looks for. XML comments cannot contain a double hyphen
  either, which is why that argument is named rather than spelled out here.
-->
<desktop:Extension Category="windows.startupTask" Executable="app\SnapAway.exe" EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">
  <desktop:StartupTask TaskId="SnapAway" Enabled="false" DisplayName="SnapAway" />
</desktop:Extension>
